        "severity": "WARNING",
        "location": "longterm-mem/internal/memorycard/store.go:146",
        "claim": "MemoryByEngramID takes only the first import_map part (order by part limit 1). An observation imported as several parts, each a different memory, has a reverse lineage naming only one memory. A page owned by another part's memory would not be recognised as bridged once the walk uses lineage, so the observation could be promoted again. If the part-1 row is dangling, the lookup reports no memory even when later parts resolve.",
        "evidence_class": "inferential",
        "causal_disposition": "introduced",
        "proof_refs": [
          "longterm-mem/internal/memorycard/store.go:146",
          "longterm-mem/cmd/longterm-mem/lineage.go:14-21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "F2",
        "severity": "SUGGESTION",
        "location": "longterm-mem/cmd/longterm-mem/lineage.go:14-21",
        "claim": "Lineage is asymmetric. A hand-mirrored memorycard memory names its Engram twin observation, but that observation names no memorycard memory, and the test pins this. A later walk that bridges by lineage identity will therefore skip the memory side and still promote the observation side. This is the same re-promotion hole the feature aims to close, left open for mirrored memories.",
        "evidence_class": "inferential",
        "causal_disposition": "introduced",
        "proof_refs": [
          "longterm-mem/cmd/longterm-mem/lineage.go:14-21",
          "longterm-mem/cmd/longterm-mem/lineage_test.go:113-115"
        ]
      },
